// TEST O — CALL / RETURN (the real test):
// Flow: JAL calls function at 0x14 (saving ra=0x08) → function sets x13=9 → JALR x0,0(x1) returns to ra (0x08) → x12=1 → JAL x0 jumps to end → x14=99.
// Expected: x1=0x08 (ra), x13=9 (function ran), x12=1 (returned to correct address), x14=99 (end), x10=0.

//00000513   # ADDI x10,x0,0        0x00 marker
//010000EF   # JAL  x1,+16          0x04 CALL func@0x14, ra(x1)=0x08
//00100613   # ADDI x12,x0,1        0x08 AFTER RETURN: x12=1
//0100006F   # JAL  x0,+16          0x0C jump past func to end@0x1C
//00000513   # ADDI x10,x0,0        0x10 filler
//00900693   # ADDI x13,x0,9        0x14 FUNCTION body: x13=9
//00008067   # JALR x0,0(x1)        0x18 RETURN to ra (0x08)
//06300713   # ADDI x14,x0,99       0x1C END: x14=99

// TEST P — mcycle increments:
// Expected: x2 > x1 (mcycle advanced), x3 = small positive (cycles elapsed between reads). 
// The exact delta depends on your pipeline depth/CPI, but the key checks: x1 ≠ x2, x2 > x1, x3 is a small positive count. 
// This proves the counter is running and readable.

//B00020F3   # CSRRS x1,mcycle,x0   x1 = mcycle @ t1 (pure read, rs1=x0)
//00100293   # ADDI  x5,x0,1        burn cycles
//00200313   # ADDI  x6,x0,2
//00300393   # ADDI  x7,x0,3
//B0002173   # CSRRS x2,mcycle,x0   x2 = mcycle @ t2
//401101B3   # SUB   x3,x2,x1       x3 = x2 - x1


// TEST Q — write mcycle, read back:
// Expected: x2 ≈ 100 + (a few cycles) — a value near 100, NOT a huge free-running number. 
// This confirms CSRRW actually wrote mcycle (overriding the free-running value). If x2 is huge/unrelated to 100, your CSRRW write isn't landing.

06400093   # ADDI  x1,x0,100
B0009073   # CSRRW x0,mcycle,x1   mcycle = 100 (rd=x0 discards old)
06400093   # ADDI  x1,x0,100
06400093   # ADDI  x1,x0,100
06400093   # ADDI  x1,x0,100
06400093   # ADDI  x1,x0,100
06400093   # ADDI  x1,x0,100
B0002173   # CSRRS x2,mcycle,x0   x2 = mcycle







